# Getting started with SpaceTAP

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:space_tap, "~> 0.3.0"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"}
])
```

## What SpaceTAP does

[TAP](https://www.ivoa.net/documents/TAP/), the IVOA Table Access Protocol, is how astronomical archives expose their tables over HTTP. You write a query in [ADQL](https://www.ivoa.net/documents/ADQL/), a dialect of SQL with functions for positions on the sky, and the service runs it and sends the table back.

`SpaceTAP.query/3` runs a sync query: one HTTP request, with the result in the response (TAP 1.1 §2.1). It POSTs the ADQL to the `/sync` endpoint of the service with `REQUEST=doQuery`, `LANG=ADQL` and `FORMAT=csv`, and reads the CSV that comes back. With `format: :votable` it asks for VOTable instead, and reads typed values. `SpaceTAP.async_query/3` runs a query as a job, for queries that take longer than one HTTP request should.

This notebook installs SpaceTAP from Hex. Every query goes to a live service, so the numbers you get may differ a little from the ones mentioned here.

## A first query

The service URL is the base URL of a TAP service, what TAP calls its access URL. This one is the GAVO Data Center, the German Virtual Observatory's archive, and the query reads its copy of Gaia DR3, `gaia.dr3lite`. The Gaia archive at ESA, `https://gea.esac.esa.int/tap-server/tap`, answers the same query on `gaia.dr3lite`. The query asks for the five brightest stars within one degree of the centre of the Pleiades:

```elixir
gavo = "https://dc.g-vo.org/tap"

adql = """
SELECT TOP 5 source_id, ra, dec, parallax, phot_g_mean_mag
FROM gaia.dr3lite
WHERE CONTAINS(POINT('ICRS', ra, dec), CIRCLE('ICRS', 56.75, 24.12, 1.0)) = 1
  AND parallax IS NOT NULL AND phot_g_mean_mag IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY phot_g_mean_mag
"""

{:ok, result} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql)
```

`CONTAINS(POINT(...), CIRCLE(...)) = 1` is how ADQL writes a cone search: it keeps the rows whose position falls inside a circle of 1° around right ascension 56.75° and declination 24.12°. `phot_g_mean_mag` is the brightness in Gaia's G band, and a smaller magnitude means a brighter star, so `ORDER BY phot_g_mean_mag` puts the brightest first.

## Columns and rows

The result is a `SpaceTAP.Result`. `columns` holds the column names in the order of the query:

```elixir
result.columns
```

Each row is a list with one value per column, in the same order:

```elixir
hd(result.rows)
```

Every value is a string, because CSV has no types. An empty cell would be `nil`. To work with maps instead, zip each row with the columns:

```elixir
stars = Enum.map(result.rows, fn row -> result.columns |> Enum.zip(row) |> Map.new() end)
```

`Kino.DataTable` shows them as a table you can sort. `keys:` keeps the columns in the order of the query, which a map doesn't:

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(stars, keys: result.columns, name: "Brightest stars in the Pleiades")
```

## From strings to numbers

To compute anything, parse the values first. Parallax is in milliarcseconds, and the distance in parsecs is roughly 1000 divided by it:

```elixir
for star <- stars do
  {parallax, ""} = Float.parse(star["parallax"])
  %{source_id: star["source_id"], distance_pc: Float.round(1000 / parallax, 1)}
end
```

`source_id` is a 64-bit integer. Keep it as a string, or use `String.to_integer/1`, but don't turn it into a float: floats can't hold every integer that large exactly.

## Typed values with VOTable

VOTable is the IVOA format for tables, and every TAP service must support it (TAP 1.1 §2.7.3). Each column is a `FIELD` that declares its datatype, so `format: :votable` gives values you can compute with:

```elixir
{:ok, result} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, adql, format: :votable)
hd(result.rows)
```

`source_id` is a `long`, so it comes as an integer, and the rest are floats. `phot_g_mean_mag` is a `float`, a 32-bit number, and SpaceTAP gives the exact value of its 32 bits, with more digits than the CSV shows: `3.6157947` there is `3.6157946586608887` here. Both are the same number.

`fields` describes each column with a `SpaceTAP.Field`:

```elixir
result.fields
|> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, [:name, :datatype, :unit, :description]))
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:name, :datatype, :unit, :description], name: "Columns")
```

No parsing is needed now:

```elixir
for [source_id, _ra, _dec, parallax, _mag] <- result.rows do
  %{source_id: source_id, distance_pc: Float.round(1000 / parallax, 1)}
end
```

CSV stays the default, so code written for strings keeps working.

## Options

`:maxrec` asks the service to return at most that many rows (DALI 1.1 §3.4.4). The service may apply a lower limit of its own.

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 10 source_id FROM gaia.dr3lite", maxrec: 3)

length(result.rows)
```

Four rows, not three: GAVO sends one more than `MAXREC` asks for, although DALI 1.1 §3.4.4 says the service "must only return the requested number of rows". SpaceTAP returns the rows the service sends.

A CSV result doesn't say whether the service cut it short. A VOTable does, with a query status of `OVERFLOW` (DALI 1.1 §4.4.1), and SpaceTAP sets `overflow`:

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 10 source_id FROM gaia.dr3lite",
    maxrec: 3,
    format: :votable
  )

result.overflow
```

With CSV, `overflow` is `nil`: if you get exactly as many rows as the limit, assume there are more.

`maxrec: 0` asks for the columns with no rows, which is a quick way to see what a query returns. GAVO sends one row anyway:

```elixir
SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 10 source_id, ra, dec FROM gaia.dr3lite", maxrec: 0)
```

`:req_options` goes to [Req](https://hexdocs.pm/req/), the HTTP client. Req waits 15 seconds for a response by default; raise it for slower queries:

```elixir
SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 1 source_id FROM gaia.dr3lite",
  req_options: [receive_timeout: 60_000]
)
```

## Async queries

A sync query's result comes in the response to a single HTTP request, so the query has to finish while that request is open. `SpaceTAP.async_query/3` runs it as a job instead (TAP 1.1 §2.2), with the options of `query/3` and the same result, and a `:timeout` for how long to wait:

```elixir
{:ok, result} = SpaceTAP.async_query(gavo, adql, format: :votable, timeout: :timer.minutes(5))
hd(result.rows)
```

The service keeps the job, with its query, its phase and its result, at a URL of its own. [UWS](https://www.ivoa.net/documents/UWS/), the IVOA protocol for jobs, says how a client creates a job, follows it and deletes it, and `SpaceTAP.Job` has each step.

`submit/3` POSTs the query to the `/async` endpoint, with the same parameters as a sync query, and the service answers with a 303 to the URL of the new job (UWS 1.1 §2.2.3.1). A new job usually waits as `PENDING` until the client sends `PHASE=RUN` (UWS 1.1 §2.2.3.5), which `submit/3` does too:

```elixir
{:ok, job} = SpaceTAP.Job.submit(gavo, adql, format: :votable)
```

By now the job is `:queued` or `:executing`, or already `:completed` if the query is small. `wait/2` asks for the job with `WAIT=30`, so the service answers when the phase changes, or after 30 seconds (UWS 1.1 §2.2.1.2), and asks again until the job stops:

```elixir
{:ok, job} = SpaceTAP.Job.wait(job, timeout: :timer.minutes(5))
```

A `:completed` job lists the URL of its result, and `result/2` reads it as `query/3` reads a sync result. GAVO lists an `http://` URL, which SpaceTAP reads with `https://`:

```elixir
{:ok, result} = SpaceTAP.Job.result(job)
length(result.rows)
```

`job.url` is all you need to come back to the job, from another process or later on, with `SpaceTAP.Job.get/2`. The job stays on the service until the client deletes it or the service destroys it, at its destruction time (UWS 1.1 §2.1.5):

```elixir
SpaceTAP.Job.delete(job)
```

`async_query/3` deletes its job whatever happens: when the job has stopped, when it fails, and when the `:timeout` runs out, which stops it.

## When a query fails

A failed query returns `{:error, %SpaceTAP.Error{}}`. A query that names a table GAVO doesn't have gives a service error, with the message GAVO wrote. GAVO's software, DaCHS, sends it with HTTP 500:

```elixir
{:error, error} = SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 3 source_id FROM gaia.no_such_table")
```

`SpaceTAP.Error` is an exception, and its message starts with the HTTP status:

```elixir
Exception.message(error)
```

`:type` says what went wrong:

| Type               | When                                                                                     |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `:service_error`   | The service rejected the query, for example because of invalid ADQL or an unknown table. |
| `:http_error`      | An HTTP error status without an error document from the service, such as a proxy's page. |
| `:transport_error` | No response arrived: the connection failed or timed out.                                 |
| `:decode_error`    | A successful response that can't be read as a result, such as one in another format.     |
| `:timeout`         | An async query's job hadn't stopped when its `:timeout` ran out.                         |

An async query whose job fails gives a `:service_error` too. The job document reports the error, not the HTTP status, so `:status` is `nil` and the message names the phase:

```elixir
{:error, error} = SpaceTAP.async_query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 3 source_id FROM gaia.no_such_table")
Exception.message(error)
```

So you can decide what to do from the type alone:

```elixir
case SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 3 source_id FROM gaia.no_such_table") do
  {:ok, result} ->
    "#{length(result.rows)} rows"

  {:error, %SpaceTAP.Error{type: :service_error, message: message}} ->
    "Fix the query: " <> (message |> String.split("\n") |> hd())

  {:error, %SpaceTAP.Error{type: type}} when type in [:http_error, :transport_error] ->
    "Try again later"

  {:error, error} ->
    raise error
end
```

